.TH FSTAB-EDIT 1 "November 2026" "fstab-edit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fstab-edit \- safe editor for fstab(5) with validation, backup and rollback
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fstab-edit
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.br
.B fstab-edit
.RI [ global-options ]
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fstab-edit
is a command-line and interactive (TUI) editor for the static file system
table
.IR fstab (5).
Every modifying operation runs in a transaction: the target file is locked,
a backup copy is created, the change is written atomically, and the result
is verified. If a post-write check fails, the target is rolled back
automatically.
.PP
Running
.B fstab-edit
without arguments opens the interactive TUI editor (same as the
.B tui
command). Read-only commands
.RB ( list ,
.BR validate ,
.BR verify-mount )
do not create a log file; mutating commands and
.B backup
require the log file to be available before the target is touched.
Machine-readable output formats print exactly one structured document to
stdout; diagnostics, diffs and prompts go to stderr.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
Show active and commented-out entries of the target file.
.TP
.B add
Add a new entry. Requires
.BR --device ,
.BR --mount ,
.BR --type
and
.BR --options ;
.B --dump
and
.B --pass
default to 0;
.B --comment
adds a comment block in front of the entry.
.TP
.BI edit " selector"
Modify selected fields of an entry. Unspecified fields are left unchanged.
Supports new
.BR --device ,
.BR --mount ,
.BR --type ,
.BR --options ,
.BR --dump ,
.BR --pass ,
.BR --comment ,
and the clearing flags
.BR --clear-comment ,
.BR --unset-dump ,
.BR --unset-pass .
.TP
.BI delete " selector"
Delete the selected entry.
.TP
.BI comment " selector"
Comment out the selected active entry; its comment block is handled together
with the entry.
.TP
.BI uncomment " selector"
Uncomment the selected commented-out entry.
.TP
.B validate
Validate the document with the built-in validator and, unless
.B --offline
is given, with an external static check
.RB ( findmnt --verify )
against a private copy of the target. Warnings are acceptable (exit 0);
errors exit 1.
.TP
.B verify-mount
Run only the static
.B findmnt --verify
check against the actual bytes of the target file.
.B findmnt
does not mount anything.
.TP
.B backup
Create a manual backup copy of the target without modifying it.
.B --dry-run
performs no I/O at all and reports an empty backup ID.
.TP
.BI rollback " [backup-id]"
Restore the target from a backup copy. The copy is selected by positional
.B backup-id
or
.BI --restore=ID
(default: newest). Before writing, a safety backup of the current target is
created and a diff is shown for confirmation.
.TP
.B tui
Open the interactive terminal UI (bubbletea) editor. Invoked automatically
when
.B fstab-edit
runs without arguments.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --file " path"
Path to the fstab file being edited (default:
.IR /etc/fstab ).
.TP
.BI --config " path"
Path to a YAML configuration file. When not given, configuration is looked
up automatically (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BI --lang " ru|en"
Language of messages. Default: resolved from the environment
.RI ( --lang
overrides
.BR FSTAB_EDIT_LANG ,
then
.BR LC_ALL ,
then
.BR LANG ;
fallback is
.IR ru ).
.TP
.BI --output " table|json|csv|tsv"\fR,\fP -o
Output format for list-style data (default:
.IR table ).
.TP
.BI --log-level " debug|info|warn|error"
Log level (default:
.IR info ).
.TP
.BI --backup-dir " path"
Directory for backup copies (default: directory of the target file).
.TP
.B --dry-run
Show the diff without writing, locking or creating backups.
.TP
.BR --yes ", " -y
Confirm an otherwise valid operation without prompting. Does not bypass
validation errors, selector ambiguity or external check failures.
.TP
.BR --verbose ", " -v
Additional diagnostics. Incompatible with
.BR --quiet .
.TP
.BR --quiet ", " -q
Suppress normal stdout; errors are still printed to stderr.
.TP
.B --offline
Disable checks against external resources.
.TP
.B --version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h
Print help for the root command or the given subcommand.
.SH ENTRY SELECTORS
The commands
.BR edit ,
.BR delete ,
.BR comment
and
.B uncomment
take a record selector: either a positional
.I id
or exactly one of the following flags. A numeric
.I id
is the physical line number of the entry; any other value is treated as a
mount point. A source (device) is only selectable via
.BR --device .
.TP
.BI --line " n"
Physical line number of the entry (positive integer).
.TP
.BI --mount-point " path"
Full decoded mount point value.
.TP
.BI --device " source"
Full decoded source value of the entry.
.PP
No match exits 1; several matches exit 8 with a list of candidate lines; in
an interactive terminal the tool offers to pick one of the candidates.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are resolved in the order: CLI flags, then
.B FSTAB_EDIT_*
environment variables, then user YAML, then system YAML, then built-in
defaults. The language and output format are not read from YAML (only from
environment and CLI).
.PP
Recognized YAML keys:
.TP
.BI fstab_path
Target file (default
.IR /etc/fstab ).
.TP
.BI backup.dir
Backup directory (default: directory of the target).
.TP
.BI backup.prefix
Backup file name prefix (default:
.IR fstab.bak ).
.TP
.BI backup.keep
How many newest backups rotation keeps (default: 10).
.TP
.BI log.file
Log file (default:
.IR /var/log/fstab-edit.log ).
.TP
.BI log.level
Log level:
.IR debug ", " info ", " warn " or " error
(default:
.IR info ).
.TP
.BI log.max_size_mb
Log rotation threshold in MiB (default: 10).
.TP
.BI log.keep
How many rotated log archives to keep (default: 5).
.TP
.BI tui.theme
TUI theme:
.IR dark ", " light ", " high-contrast " or " monochrome
(default:
.IR dark ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/fstab-edit/config.yaml
Per-user configuration. Not loaded automatically for a root (euid 0)
process.
.TP
.B /etc/fstab-edit/config.yaml
System-wide configuration; must be owned by root.
.TP
.B /var/log/fstab-edit.log
Operation log, created automatically on the first mutating operation.
.TP
.IR backup-dir /fstab.bak. <backup-id>
Backup copies of the target file. Each copy has a JSON sidecar file
(with a
.B .json
suffix) holding metadata and a SHA-256 hash.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (warnings are acceptable).
.TP
.B 1
Validation errors: invalid input data, document or change; selector matched
no entries.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: invalid arguments or flags, malformed configuration.
.TP
.B 3
Insufficient permissions: unsafe configuration file, unavailable log file.
.TP
.B 4
Access conflict: the target changed between load and write, or the lock is
unavailable.
.TP
.B 5
External check failure (e.g. findmnt(8) missing or failed).
.TP
.B 6
I/O error: backup, write or rotation failure.
.TP
.B 7
Critical rollback failure: the target was written but could not be restored.
.TP
.B 8
Ambiguous selector: several entries match; candidate line numbers are listed.
.SH EXAMPLES
List entries of a test file in JSON format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fstab-edit list --file ./fstab.test -o json | jq .
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview adding an entry (diff only, nothing is written):
.PP
.RS
.nf
fstab-edit add --device UUID=3f5c8a12-9d4e-4b7a-8c1f-2a6d9e0b4f55 \e
    --mount /mnt/data --type ext4 --options defaults,noatime \e
    --dump 0 --pass 2 --comment "data disk" --dry-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a backup and roll back to the newest copy, non-interactively:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo fstab-edit backup
sudo fstab-edit rollback --yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open the interactive editor:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo fstab-edit tui
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fstab (5),
.BR mount (8),
.BR findmnt (8),
.BR lsblk (8)
